.TH RATIONALS 3 "2026-10-10" "fortran_rationals 0.2.0" "Fortran Library Functions"
.SH NAME
rationals \- exact rational numbers with checked 64-bit arithmetic
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B use rationals
.B type(rational) :: r
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Module
.B rationals
provides the derived type
.BR rational ,
a fraction num/den with int64 parts, always in lowest terms with den > 0.
The components are private.
.PP
Construct with
.BR rational ( n ", " d )
(both int32 or both int64),
.BR rational ( n )
(int32),
.BR rational ( x )
(real64: the simplest fraction that converts back to exactly x),
or
.BR rational ( \(dq3/4\(dq )
(text "n", "n/d" or a decimal real; blanks are allowed around the parts,
any other text such as "1/2/3" or "1 2" is an error).
Assignment from a rational, an int32 or a real64 also works.
.SS Operators
.B + \- * / ** == /= < > <= >=
between rationals and int32 integers;
.B **
takes an int32 exponent.
All are elemental.
.B ==
is exact.
Comparisons never overflow.
.SS Type-bound procedures
.TP
.BR r%get_num "(), " r%get_den ()
Numerator and denominator, int64.
.TP
.BR r%is_integer "(), " r%inverse "(), " "call r%simplify" ()
den = 1; reciprocal; reduce to lowest terms (a no-op on library results).
.SS Functions
All elemental except the array functions.
.TP
.BR char ( r "), " int ( r "), " real ( r )
Text "n/d" or "n" (character(41), left-justified); int32 truncated toward zero; real64.
.TP
.BR floor ", " ceiling ", " trunc ", " round
int64; round goes half away from zero.
.TP
.BR abs ", " sign ( a ", " b "), " min ( a ", " b "), " max ( a ", " b )
As the intrinsics, for two rationals.
.TP
.BR mod ( a ", " b "), " "call divmod" ( a ", " b ", " q ", " r )
a \- trunc(a/b)*b; quotient q (int64) and remainder r together.
.TP
.BR compare ( a ", " b )
\-1, 0 or 1.
.TP
.BR gcf ( a ", " b "), " lcm ( a ", " b )
Greatest common factor and least common multiple of two int32 or two int64
integers; gcf also of two rationals.
.TP
.BR sum ( v "), " product ( v "), " dot_product ( u ", " v "), " matmul ( a ", " b "), " transpose ( a )
Rank-1 sum and product; matrix-matrix and matrix-vector products.
.TP
.BR "call random_number" ( r "), " "call set_order" ( n )
Random rational in [0, 1] whose denominator divides n (default 10).
.SS Input and output
.B print *, r
and
.B write(u, \(aq(dt)\(aq) r
write the same text as
.BR trim(char(r)) ;
list-directed output starts with a blank.
.B read(u, \(aq(dt)\(aq) r
reads one token delimited by blanks, commas or the end of the record.
.SH NOTES
Integer overflow, division by zero, unparsable text and reals out of the
int64 range stop the program with
.B error stop
and a message such as "rational: integer overflow".
Errors cannot be caught. A failed derived-type read returns iostat > 0.
.PP
Long computations grow denominators quickly; there is no arbitrary-precision
fallback.
.PP
.B random_number
uses module state set by
.BR set_order ;
it is not thread safe.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
use rationals
type(rational) :: a
a = rational(2, 3) + rational(\(dq3/4\(dq)
print *, a, real(a)        ! 17/12  1.4166666666666667
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The complete reference is llms.txt in the source distribution and
https://willklausler.github.io/fortran_rationals/
